To prove to you that modern technology has also made small-farms more efficient, you can see me in this video using a variety of technologies to produce eggs.

Eggs have protein. Protein has nitrogen in it. If you want the chickens to make lots of eggs, you need to feed them enough nitrogen containing foods. Nitrogen fertilizer costs about $2 a kilo. 1 kilo of nitrogen fertilizer is enough to produce about 450 eggs. The Haber process, which is how we produce nitrogen fertilizer, was invented in 1909. Since then it has been much easier for small farmers to produce food.

Chickens can't eat nitrogen fertilizer. You need to feed the fertilizer to a plant first, so that the plants make leaves for the chicken to eat. One of the best plants available for this purpose is elephant grass. You can see me cutting elephant grass in this video. Elephant grass became available in the 1920's, and since then it has been much easier to produce food on small scales.

Cutting plants like elephant grass was challenging for most of human history, but you can see I am using a machete in this video. A hand-full of inventions in the last 3 centuries have made modern steel tools like machetes almost free. The machete in this video cost less than $10. This makes it far easier to grow food on small scales.

Elephant grass has thick parts that wont fit into the mouth of a chicken. That is why you can see me in the video passing the elephant grass through an industrial shredder. Once the plant is shredded, chickens can eat all parts of it. This small scale shredder works great for a small farm. This kind of machine costs about $500. It has few moving parts, so maintenance is easy.







Since monocrop factory-farms are so inefficient, why have they become the standard way to produce food?
Fault lies with a misunderstood piece of technology called "land registries". Which are a kind of database that is used to remember who owns which land.

Land registries are expensive.
They are complicated, so only trained experts can edit and maintain them.
It is expensive to train the expert, and they are expensive to employ. They need to paid enough so that they don't attempt to steal land from the registry. There needs to be lots of costly security checks, so that if an expert does try to steal, they will get caught.

Every plot of land recorded in the registry is an ongoing cost that the government needs to pay. The government earns money with taxes to offset this cost.
Very large or valuable land plots pay more in tax in comparison to the cost of storing them in the registry. They are cost effective.
Small low-valued land plots do not pay enough in taxes to cover the cost of maintaining them in the registry. They are not cost effective.

In general, the low-valued land plots do not receive any land registry service. This destroys the value of these land plots. People who do not have a secure legal claim to a piece of land, will not take the risk of investing in that land. They could lose their entire investment without warning. So, the lack of land registry service causes land to lose all of its value.

